����������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���� ������������ ��������� ����� ����� ������� ���� ������� �������������� ������\n����������������������������������������������������������� ��\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������� ����������� ������ �������� �������� ������� ������������� ���� ����������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������\n�\n�\n�\n�\n�\n�\n�\n\n3 \n \n�������� �\n����������������������������������������������������������������������\n������ ����������������������� ���������������������������\n�� ��������������� ���\n�� ������������������ ��\n�� ������������������� ��\n�� ����� � ���\n�� ������ ��\n�� ������������ ��\n�� ������������� ���\n�� ��������������������� ��\n�� ������� ��\n��� ������������� ��\n��� ��� � ��\n��� �������� ���\n��� �������� ��\n��� ����������������� ��\n��� ����������������� ��\n��� ���������� ���\n��� ���������� ���\n��� �������� ���\n��� �������� ��\n��� ������������� ��\n��� � �������������� ��\n��� ������������� ����\n��� �������� ��\n��� ������������ ��\n��� ������� � ��\n��� ���������� ��\n��� ������� ���\n��� ������������ ��\n��� ������� ��\n��� ���������� ��\n��� ������� ��\n��� ����������� ���\n��� ����������� ���\n��� �������� ���\n��� �������������� ����\n��� ������������ ��\n��� ������������� ���\n� ������� ����\n���������",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_101": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 101 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 29.11.2021 \n \nFunds for Schools \n \n101. SHRI S. JAGATHRAKSHAKAN: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government agrees with the view that along with physical or \ninfrastructural investments, schools must allocate dedicated resources and adopt to \ntrain the trainer approach to make the learning lasting;  \n \n(b) if so, the measures taken by the Government in this regard; and  \n \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) to (c): Samagra Shiksha is an overarching programme  for the school \neducation sector extending from pre -school to class XII and aims to ensure inclusive \nand equitable quality education at all levels of school education. The Integrated \nScheme launched in 2018 -19, envisages the ‘school’ as a continuum from pr e-\nschool, primary, upper primary, secondary to senior secondary levels. The scheme \nnot only provides support for the implementation of the RTE Act but has also been \naligned with the recommendations of NEP 2020 to ensure that all children have \naccess to qua lity education with an equitable and inclusive classroom environment \nwhich should take care of their diverse background, multilingual needs, different \nacademic abilities and make them active participants in the learning process. \n \nThe following major steps have been taken  by the Government  to improve \nquality of education:-  \n \n1. Central RTE Rules have been amended on 20th February, 2017 to include \nreference on class-wise, subject-wise Learning Outcomes at Elementary level. \n  \n\n  \n2. A 70 indicator based matrix Perform ance Grading Index (PGI) has been \ndeveloped to grade the States and UTs.  \n3. National Initiative for Proficiency in Reading with Understanding and \nNumeracy (NIPUN Bharat) has been launched for ensuring that every child in \nthe country necessarily attains found ational literacy and numeracy (FLN) by \nthe end of Grade 3. \n4. Codification of Learning Outcomes has been developed for the foundational \nstage. \n5. Learning Outcomes for Secondary level have been notified.  \n6. An initiative has been launched to improve learning outco mes at the \nElementary level through an Integrated Teacher Training Programme called \nNISHTHA under the Centrally Sponsored Scheme of Samagra Shiksha in \nAugust 2019. The aim of this training is to motivate and equip teachers to \nencourage and foster critical thinking in students, handle diverse situations \nand act as first level counsellors. \n \nDue to the COVID pandemic and in order to provide continuous \nlearning opportunities to the teachers at the elementary level, NISHTHA online \nwas launched using DIKSHA platf orm in October 2020 to reach out to the \nremaining elementary teachers. NISHTHA online for elementary teachers \ncovers 18 courses in 11 languages. Teachers were oriented on various \naspects related to Learning Outcomes, Competency Based Learning and \nTesting, Learner-centered Pedagogy, School Safety and Security, Personal -\nsocial qualities, Inclusive Education, ICT in teaching -learning including \nArtificial Intelligence, Health and well -being including yoga, new Initiatives in \nSchool Education including library, eco club, youth club, kitchen garden, \nSchool Leadership qualities, Environmental Concerns, Pre -school, Pre -\nvocational Education and School Based Assessment in a joyful learning \nmanner. \n7. NISHTHA 2.0 for Secondary/Senior Secondary level teachers with 66 modules \nwas launched on 29th July 2021. \n8. NISHTHA 3.0 on Foundational Literacy and Numeracy with 12 modules for \nPrimary level teachers was launched on 7th September 2021.  \n\n9. Support is provided under Samagra Shiksha for improvement of quality of \neducation for various interventions such as  (i) Universal Access including \nInfrastructure Development and Retention; (ii) Foundational Literacy and \nNumeracy, (iii) Gender and Equity; (iv) Inclusive Education; (v) Quality and \nInnovation; (vi) Financial support for Teache r Salary; (vii) Digital initiatives; \n(viii) RTE Entitlements including uniforms, textbooks etc.; (ix) Support for \nECCE; (x) Vocational Education; (xi) Sports and Physical Education; (xii) \nStrengthening of Teacher Education and Training; (xiii) Monitoring; (xiv) \nProgramme Management; and (xv) National Component. \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_102": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 102 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 29.11.2021 \n \nIdentification of cities to control air pollution \n \n102. DR. G. RANJITH REDDY: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  whether it is true that Patancheruvu in Telangana has been identified to control air \npollution; \n(b)  the details of action plan prepared and status of implementation of the same; \n(c)  the details of improvement in air quality after implementation of the action plan; and \n(d)  whether any other city has been identified/ proposed to be identified to control air \npollution in Telangana and if so, the details thereof?  \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a) to (d): National Clean Air Programme (NCAP) has been launched as a comprehensive \nplan to tackle air pollution problem across the country in a focussed manner to achieve 20% \nto 30% reduction in PM10 and PM2.5 levels by 2024 from 2017 levels. Further, 42 million \nplus population cities have been identified for focused air quality management under 15th \nFinance Commission grants and out of these 34 are million plus non-attainment cities and 08 \nare million plus urban agglomerations. City Specific Air Action Plans have been prepared \nand rolled out for implementation in 132 non-attainment and million plus population cities \nincluding Hyderabad, Patancheruvu, Sangareddy and Nalgonda in State of Telangana. The \ndetails of the approved city action plan are available at https://cpcb.nic.in/approved-city-\naction-plans.  \n \nThere is a gradual improvement in the air quality in Patancheruvu as the PM10 \nreduced form 101 in 2014 to 87 in 2021. \n \n*** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_103": "3rd POSITION \nGovernment of India                                 \nMinistry of Finance \nDepartment of Financial services \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No.*103 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06th DECEMBER, 2021/ AGRAHAYANA 15, 1943 (SAKA) \n \nDisbursement under PMMY \n \n*103 SHRI SAUMITRA KHAN: \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \na) the present status of disbursement of loans under the Pradhan Mantri Mudra Yojana \n(PMMY) in West Bengal; \n(b) the number and details of beneficiaries under the scheme till April, 2021; \n(c) whether any specific targets in this regard have been fixed in respect of West Bengal: \nand \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \n FINANCE MINISTER \n (Smt. Nirmala Sitharaman) \n \n \n(a) to (d):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n****** \n \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN REPLY TO THE LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. \n*103 TO BE ANSWERED ON 06th DECEMBER, 2021 BY SHRI SAUMITRA KHAN  \nREGARDING DISBURSEMENT UNDER PMMY \n \n(a)   As on 29.10.2021, over 3.17 crore loans amounting to Rs. 1.33 lakh crore have been \ndisbursed in the State of West Bengal under Pradhan Mantri Mudra Yojana (PMMY), since \ninception of the Scheme in April, 2015. \n \n(b)    Till  April, 2021 over 29.56 crore loans amounting to Rs. 15.52 lakh crore have been \nsanctioned under the Scheme. \n \n(c) and (d)     The Government only allocates annual targets for the amount to be sanctioned \nby the Member Lending Institutions (MLIs) i.e. Scheduled Commercial Banks (SCBs), \nRegional Rural Banks (RRBs), Non -Banking Financial Companies (NBFCs) and Micro \nFinance Institutions (MFIs) under PMMY. For the current financial year (FY), target of Rs. \n3.00 lakh crore has been fixed for MLIs, under the Scheme. \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_104": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF REVENUE \n***** \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.104 \n  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY THE 29TH  NOVEMBER, 2021 \n                                                      AGRAHAYANA 8, 1943 (SAKA) \n \nCOLLECTION OF EXCISE DUTY \n \n104.  SHRI ADHIKARI DEEPAK (DEV): \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the total collection of excise duty from fuel in last five years; and  \n(b)  the total amount of excise duty collected from  Petrol and Diesel shared with \nStates in these last five years? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a):    The total Central Excise Duty, including cesses, collected from fuel, from April, \n2016 to March, 2021 is about Rs. 11.74 lakh crores. \n(b):   Tax-wise share in Central Taxes and Duties to State Government is released every \nmonth in accordance with approved recommendations made by the Finance \nCommission. Product-wise devolution of taxes and duties is not made. \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_105": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 105 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 29/11/2021 \n \nNATIONAL SKILL TRAINING INSTITUTE (NSTI) \n105. SHRI RAM MOHAN NAIDU KINJARAPU : \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP  be \npleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of National Skill Training Institut es (NSTI) and NSTI(W) in the State of \nAndhra Pradesh (AP) and the number of people trained therein since 2017, Year-wise; \n \n(b) whether there are no such institutes in the Sta te of Andhra Pradesh if so, the \nreasons therefore and whether the Government intend s to open such Institutes in the \nstate; \n \n(c) the state-wise number of individuals trained un der PMKVY 2.0 and PMKVY 3.0 \nsince the launch of these schemes; \n \n(d) the details of the number of individuals traine d in AP under PMKVY since 2017, \ndistrict-wise; \n \n(e) the details of the funds allocated, utilization  for PMKVY since 2019-20; and \n \n(f) whether the Government has undertaken any speci al initiatives for the skill \ndevelopment of the tribal population in the state of Andhra Pradesh? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTRY OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \na) Sir, there are33National Skill Training Institut es (NSTIs) spread in 19 States/UTs. \nOut of 33 NSTIs, 14 NSTIs are general and 19 NSTIs are exclusively for women. There \nis no NSTI in many states including the State of Andhra Pradesh. \n \nb) All NSTIs impart comprehensive instructor training, includes hand-on -skills and \ntraining methodology for which trainees are selecte d through a national level entrance \ntest, in which eligible trainees of all states including A ndhra Pradesh can participate. \n \nc) The state-wise number of individuals trained und er PMKVY 2.0 and PMKVY 3.0 \nis placed in Annexure I. \n \n\nd) The details of the number of individuals trained  in Andhra Pradesh under PMKVY \n