    "ls|LS17_S7_U_172": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.172 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 29TH NOVEMBER, 2021 \n \nPROBLEM OF UNEMPLOYMENT \n \n172.  KUNWAR DANISH ALI: \n \n Will the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether it is a fact that the problem of  unemployment has aggravated in the \ncountry during the last five years; \n(b) if so, the details of the unemployment rate during the said period, year-wise; and \n(c) the number of unemployed that have been  provided employment during th e last \nfive years and the  steps being taken by the Government to provide  employment \nto the unemployed? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) to (c): The data on employment/ unemployment is collected through Periodic  \nLabour Force Survey (PLFS) conducted by National Statistical Office (NSO), Ministry of \nStatistics and Programme Implementation (MoSPI) since 2017-18. As per the results of \nPLFS conducted during 2017 -18, 2018 -19 and 2019 -20, the details of the estimated \nWorker Population Ratio (WPR) and the estimated Unemployment Rate (UR) for \npersons of 15 years and above on usual status basis in the country is given below: \nYear WPR(%) UR(%) \n2017-18 46.8 6.0 \n2018-19 47.3 5.8 \n2019-20 50.9 4.8 \n \n \n\nEmployment generation is the priority of the Government. Government of India \nhas taken a number of initiatives for promoting employment generation in the country. \nAatmanirbhar Bharat RojgarYojana (ABRY) was launched with effect from 1st October, \n2020 as part of Aatmanirbhar Bharat package 3.0 to incentivize employers for creation \nof new employment along with social security benefits and restoration of loss of \nemployment during Covid -19 pandemic. This scheme being implemented through \nEmployees’ Provident Fund Organisation (EPFO), seeks to reduce the financial burden \nof the employers and encourages them to hire more workers. The terminal date for \nregistration of beneficiaries has been extended from 30.06.2021 to 31.03.2022. As on \n20.11.2021, benefit has been p rovided to 39.43 lakh beneficiaries through 1.15 lakh \nestablishments. \n            PM-SVANidhi Scheme has facilitated collateral free working capital loan upto \nRs.10,000/- for one -year tenure to street vendors, to help them  resume their \nbusinesses. \n            Pradhan Mantri Mudra Yojana (PMMY) is being implemented by the \nGovernment, inter alia, for facilitating self -employment. Under PMMY collateral free \nloans uptoRs. 10 lakh are extended to micro/small business enterprises and to \nindividuals to enable them to setup or expand their business activities. \n              In addition, to enhance the employment generation, Government is \nencouraging various projects involving substantial investment and through public \nexpenditure on schemes like Prime Minister’s Emp loyment Generation Programme \n(PMEGP), Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme \n(MGNREGS), Pt. Deen  Dayal Upadhyaya Grameen Kaushalya Yojana (DDU-GKY), Deen \nDayal Antodaya Yojana-National Urban Livelihoods Mission (DAY -NULM) and Pradhan \nMantri Kaushal Vikas  Yojana (PMKVY) run by Ministry of Micro, Small & Medium \nEnterprises, Ministry of Rural Development, Ministry of Housing & Urban Affairs and \nMinistry of Skill Development & Entrepreneurship (MSDE), respectively. \n              Besides these initiatives, the flagship programmes of the Government such as \nMake in India, Digital India, Smart City Mission, Atal Mission for Rejuvenation and \nUrban Transformation, Housing for All, Infrastructure development and Industrial \ncorridors and Production-Linked Incentive (PLI) Scheme are also oriented to generate \nproductive employment opportunities. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_173": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 173 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 09, 2021  \nIMPLEMENTATION OF SWACHH BHARAT MISSION \n \nNO. 173.   SHRI JAGDAMBIKA PAL:  \n    \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be p leased to \nstate: \n    \n(a)  whether any competition has been held for moti vating the  \n  cities to   accelerate  implementation of  Swachh   Bharat  \n  Mission (SBM) in addition to funding support and if so, the  \n  details thereof;  \n(b)  the cities which have achieved top ranking dur ing the last  \n  two years;  \n(c)  whether  any  incentive  or  reward  is  given   to  the cities  \n  performing well and if so, the details thereof;  \n(d)  whether there are any technology interventions  to digitize  \n  and  accelerate  the  implementation of SBM and i f so, the  \n  details  thereof  along  with  the  details of im pact of such  \n  technological interventions;  \n(e)  whether any parameters have been identified to  evaluate the \n  Solid  Waste  Management  in  a  city  and  if  s o, the details \n  thereof; and  \n(f)  whether  there   are  any   parameters  to  as sess  the  open \n  defecation and sanitation status in the urban are as and if so, \n  the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI  HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a)  to (f)     A Statement is laid on the table of the House \n \n***** \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT  \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (f) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION No.173 FOR ANSWER ON DECEMBE R, 9, \n2021 REGARDING IMPLEMENTATION OF SWACHH BHARAT MISSION. \n \n(a) : In addition to the funding support, a nationa l level sanitation \nsurvey (Swachh Survekshan) is conducted annually to  motivate cities \nto accelerate implementation of Swachh Bharat Mission-Urban (SBM-U). \nDetails of cities participation in the Swachh Surve kshans since its \nlaunch in 2016 is as under : \n \nSl. No. Year Number of Cities participated \n1. 2016 73 \n2. 2017 434 \n3. 2018 4,203 \n4. 2019 4,237 \n5. 2020 4,242 \n6. 2021 4,320 \n \nThe seventh ‘Swachh Survekshan (SS)-2022’, titled ‘Azadi@75’, has been \nlaunched on September 27, 2021.  \n \n(b): The details of the top ranking ULBs during the  last two years are as \nunder: \n \nAll India \nposition \nTop ranking Urban Local Bodies (ULBs) \nSwachh Survekshan, 2020  Swachh Survekshan, 2021 \n1st  rank Indore, Madhya Pradesh Indore, Madhya Pradesh  \n2nd  rank Surat, Gujarat Surat, Gujarat \n3rd  rank Navi Mumbai, Maharashtra  Vijayawada, Andhra Pradesh  \n \n(c):  Cities performance in Swachh Survekshan is re cognised and \nrewarded in a national level felicitation ceremony.  List of such award \ncategories in SS-2021 is annexed. \n(d):  Various digital interventions have been intro duced to digitize and \naccelerate the implementation of SBM-U. Key interve ntions are as \nunder : \n(i). Swachhata App: On this App citizens can regist er their \ncomplaints about ‘Swachhata’ to ensure that ULB off icials resolve \nthese issues.  Till date more than 1.80 crore citiz ens have \ndownloaded the Swachhata App, registering more than  2.40 crore \ncomplaints, out of which 2.24 crore complaints have  been \nresolved. \n…3/- \n\n \n \n-3- \n \n(ii). SBM Toilet Locator: To provide easy access to  Community/ \nPublic Toilets (CT/PT), more than 66,000 toilet blo cks have been \nuploaded across 3,296 ULBs.  The users can also reg ister their \nfeedbacks on the maintenance of the toilets in the in-built \nfeedback machine in the toilet premises, which help  ULBs in \nmonitoring cleanliness, maintenance and upkeep of the toilets. \n(iii) Swachhatam Portal : The single destination Sw achhata \nManagement Portal, launched on November 20, 2021 un der SBM-U \n2.0 serves as the single data source for the ULBs t o upload their \nupdated progress, prepare their action plans, submi t the project \nproposals for funding, project tracking, Star Ratin g for Garbage \nfree Cities and ODF (Open Defecation Free) Protocol  etc., for the \nassessment agencies to validate the same from citiz ens and field \nverifications during annual assessments. \n(e): The assessment for evaluating Solid Waste Mana gement (SWM) \nunder the Swachh Survekshan is primarily made on th e basis of certain \nService Level Indicators (SLIs) which includes adop tion of the practice \nof door-to-door collection of waste, segregation at  source, \ntransportation of waste, scientific processing of t he waste through \nvarious methods which involve material recovery fac ilities (MRFs), \nwaste to compost (WtC), Waste to Energy (WtE), Biom ethanation and \nrecycling of Construction and Demolition (C&D) wast e and the final \ndisposal through sanitary landfills and other metho ds. Sustainable \nSanitation and SafaiMitra Suraksha have also a bear ing during the \nassessment. The Survekshan also has a ‘people first ’ approach and \ntherefore citizens’ voice is captured during the assessment.  \n(f) : To assess the ODF and sanitation status Stand ard Operating \nProtocols (SOPs) have been developed for certification of ULBs as Open \nDefecation Free (ODF), ODF+, ODF++ and Water+ status. \n \nA city can be declared ODF if at any point of the d ay, not even a single \nperson is found defecating in the open. \n \n \n…4/- \n\n \n \n-4- \n \nParameter for declaring a city ODF+ is that at any point of the day, not \neven a single person is found defecating and / or u rinating in the open, \nand all community and public toilets are functional , hygienic and well \nmaintained. \n \nFor ODF++ status, the parameter is that the city, i n addition to ODF+, \nshould also comply the condition that faecal sludge / septage and \nsewerage is safely managed and treated, with no dis charging and /or \ndumping of untreated faecal sludge/ septage and sew erage in drains, \nwater bodies or open areas. \n \nParameter of Water+ status is that a city should have met all the ODF++ \nstandards and also the residual sewage from residen tial and \ncommercial establishments is released into the envi ronment only after \ntreatment.  \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n\n \n \n \nANNEXURE AS REFERRED IN REPLY TO PART(C) OF LOK SAB HA STARRED QUESTION \nNO.173 DUE FOR REPLY ON 09.12.2021 REGARDING ‘IMPLE MENTATION OF SWACHH \nBHARAT MISSION”  \nS. No.  Level Award  \n  More than 1 lakh Population \n1 National  India's Cleanest City    -  No.1   \n2 National  India's Cleanest City    -  No.2  \n3 National  India's Cleanest City    -  No.3  \n4 National  Best City in 'Maximum Citizens Participation'  \n  More than 40 lakh Population \n5 National  India's 'Cleanest Mega City  \n6 National   'Fastest Mover' Mega City  \n7 National  Best Mega City in 'Citizens Feedback'  \n8 National  Best Mega City in 'Innovation & Best Practices'  \n9 National  Best 'Self -Sustainable City'  \n  10 -40 lakh Population  \n10  National  India's 'Cleanest big City'  \n11  National  Fastest Mover' big City  \n12  National  Best Big City in 'Citizens Feedback'  \n13  National  Best Big City in 'Innovation & Best Practices'  \n14  National  Best 'Self -Sustainable Big City'  \n  3-10 lakh Population  \n15  National  India's 'Cleanest Medium City'  \n16  National  Fastest Mover' Medium City  \n17  National  Best Medium City in 'Citizens Feedback'  \n18  National  Best Medium City in 'Innovation & Best Practices'  \n19  National  Best 'Self -Sustainable Medium City'  \n  1-3 lakh Population  \n20  National  India's 'Cleanest Small City'  \n21  National  Fastest Mover' Small City  \n22  National  Best Small City in 'Citizens Feedback'  \n23  National  Best Small City in 'Innovation & Best Practices'  \n24  National  Best 'Self -Sustainable Small City'  \n  State Capitals / UT's  \n25  National  India's 'Cleanest State Capital/UT'  \n26  National  Fastest Mover' State Capital/UT  \n27  National  Best State Capital in 'Citizens F